aiting-time imported as a requirement; sample emergency records showing triage applied at arrival against the unique identification number, with the category, the time of assignment and the person who assigned it, and showing life-threatening presentation was not queued; sample records showing the category changed what happened next — pathway, clinician called, space used — rather than a stamp followed by a single queue; the written distinction that this system guides initiation of appropriate emergency care and is not AAC.2.e's hospital-wide access prioritisation, even where the local tool is shared, and is not the AAC.3 emergency initial-assessment dataset and is not AAC.3.e's ward early-warning method; records of re-triage when a waiting patient's condition changed, using step 4; the location of the written triage guidance; briefing records of the staff who triage; the audit sample at step 12 of triage applied and recorded rather than a single queue", "responsible": "Staff who triage apply the system at arrival and re-apply it when status changes; person in charge of the emergency area holds the written guidance; clinicians start care at the category assigned; quality or accreditation coordinator audits"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.d", "requirement": "Patients waiting in the emergency are reassessed as appropriate for the change in status.", "steps": "Steps 4, 3, 12", "evidence": "The written reassessment interval for each triage category while waiting; sample records of waiting patients reassessed against the unique identification number, with category unchanged or re-triaged; the audit sample at step 12 of waiting-patient reassessment rather than a first stamp left untouched", "responsible": "Staff who triage and observe waiting patients reassess at the stated interval; person in charge of the emergency area holds the interval; quality or accreditation coordinator audits"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.e", "requirement": "Admission, discharge to home or transfer to another organization is documented, and a discharge note shall be given to the patient.", "steps": "Steps 5, 12", "evidence": "The written emergency discharge-note content and who gives it; sample notes given at admission from the emergency area, at discharge to home, at transfer to another organisation and at leaving against medical advice from the emergency area, each carrying the unique identification number; records showing the AAC.2 transfer note used in addition when transfer was between organisations, and the AAC.8 summary used in addition when the patient later left the organisation from a ward; the audit sample at step 12 of an emergency discharge note given at disposition", "responsible": "Clinicians document disposition and give the emergency discharge note; AAC.2 owns the between-organisation transfer note; AAC.8 owns the leaving-the-organisation summary; quality or accreditation coordinator audits"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.f", "requirement": "The organization shall implement a quality assurance programme.", "steps": "Steps 6, 9, 11, 12", "evidence": "The named emergency quality lead and the accountability of that role for bringing a finding that care does not match this document to the head of the institution; the written emergency quality-assurance indicators and the method of measurement, showing a local set that is actually measured — including whether triage is applied and recorded, whether waiting patients are reassessed, whether medico-legal documentation is complete and contemporaneous, whether the emergency discharge note is given, whether the ambulance checklist is done, and whether the disaster plan has been drilled — rather than a copied list never reviewed, and showing no mandated turnaround-time minutes, left-without-being-seen rate or staffing ratio as a NABH requirement; records of measurement against those indicators; the forum and interval at which findings are reviewed, and review records that include indicators defined and actually discussed; documented corrective action when a finding showed a gap, rather than filing without a change in practice; records showing this programme is the emergency service's and is not treated as a substitute for laboratory, imaging, infection-surveillance or intensive-care quality-assurance programmes; ambulance equipment and medication checks at step 9 included as inputs to this programme; disaster-plan drill records at step 11 included as inputs to this programme; the audit sample at step 12 of indicators measured and reviewed rather than listed", "responsible": "Named emergency quality lead operates the programme, records findings and corrective action, and reports; person in charge of the emergency area is accountable that the programme is run; quality or accreditation coordinator audits"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.g", "requirement": "The organization has systems in place for the management of patients found dead on arrival and patients who die within a few minutes of arrival.", "steps": "Steps 7, 2, 12", "evidence": "The written dead-on-arrival and death-within-minutes procedure naming who confirms death, identity when unknown, family communication, police intimation when medico-legal, movement and holding of the body, and the record against a unique identification number; sample records of registration of a dead-on-arrival patient rather than an unregistered body; the audit sample at step 12 of dead-on-arrival records that show a system", "responsible": "Person in charge of the emergency area holds the procedure; clinicians confirm death and apply step 2 when the case is medico-legal; quality or accreditation coordinator audits"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.h", "requirement": "The organization has access to ambulance services commensurate with the scope of services provided by it.", "steps": "Steps 8, 12", "evidence": "The written arrangement for accessing ambulance services — owned, contracted, or a named provider — showing alignment with the defined service directory; records of actual access rather than a telephone number that does not produce a vehicle; the audit sample at step 12 of ambulance access", "responsible": "Person in charge of the emergency area and administration hold the access arrangement; head of the institution is accountable that ambulance access matches the defined scope"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.i", "requirement": "The ambulance(s) is fit for purpose, is operated by trained personnel, is appropriately equipped, and ensures that emergency medications are available in the ambulance.", "steps": "Steps 9, 6, 12", "evidence": "The written ambulance equipment and emergency-medication list and the named trained roles that operate the ambulance; check records showing equipment and medications present and usable at the stated interval, rather than a sealed cupboard never opened; human-resource verification of those personnel used, not restated; the audit sample at step 12 of ambulance checks and medications present", "responsible": "Ambulance personnel complete the check; person in charge of the emergency area holds the list; medication policies own the medication process when issued; quality or accreditation coordinator audits"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.j", "requirement": "The emergency department identifies opportunities to initiate treatment at the earliest, when the patient is in transit to the organization.", "steps": "Steps 10, 8, 12", "evidence": "The written method by which the emergency department initiates treatment while the patient is in transit; sample records of in-transit treatment handed over on arrival against the unique identification number; the audit sample at step 12 of in-transit treatment where it was identified", "responsible": "Emergency clinicians identify and advise in-transit treatment; ambulance personnel apply the standing method and hand over on arrival; quality or accreditation coordinator audits"}, {"oe_code": "COP.2.k", "requirement": "The organization manages potential community emergencies, epidemics and other disasters as per a documented plan.", "steps": "Steps 11, 6, 12", "evidence": "The documented all-hazards operational plan covering community emergencies, epidemics and other disasters, naming who has authority to activate it including out of hours, the operational command structure by role, how arriving patients are sorted under disaster conditions, how surge capacity would be created, which clinical services are essential and how they continue, how the hospital communicates with staff, families and district or municipal authorities, and how the plan is stood down; the written method for creating surge capacity and continuing essential services, with no mandated extra-bed percentage, staffing ratio or hours of oxygen as a NABH requirement; records showing National Disaster Management Authority guidelines used as a framework if chosen, not as an imported numeric checklist; records showing the Disaster Management Act, 2005 and the Epidemic Diseases Act, 1897 as amended 2020 cited as the statutes under which external directions may bind the hospital; the written division that this plan is operational command, surge, disaster triage and continuation of essential services, while HIC.1.d owns statutory notification, IEC and the IPC outbreak-and-pandemic response, including that HIC.1 step 24 is not a substitute for this plan, and that the two documents name the same epidemic-activation role or state how the two activations join; facility fire and building-evacuation left to facility-management policies, with this plan owning clinical command during those events; drill or tabletop records at the stated interval, with participation, findings and resulting revision, rather than a binder never tested; quality-assurance review of whether the plan has been drilled, under step 6; the audit sample at step 12 of a disaster plan that has been drilled", "responsible": "The role named as activator activates, commands and stands down the plan; department heads continue essential services the plan names; Infection Control Team owns HIC.1 notification and IPC outbreak response, not this operational command unless named in both documents; named emergency quality lead includes drills in the quality-assurance review; quality or accreditation coordinator audits; head of the institution is accountable that a documented plan exists and can be activated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  $q$Universal (non-NABH) facts included in this draft, and where each was verified. Check these first.

SOURCE OF THE OE TEXT
0. COP.2 standard text and all eleven OEs were read directly from the official NABH SHCO Standards 3rd Edition PDF (August 2022), Chapter 2 Care of Patients, printed pages 61-62 (PDF page index 67-68). The PDF was downloaded on 2026-08-17 from the NABH website's Explore NABH Standards page. Levels: COP.2.a Commitment, COP.2.b Core, COP.2.c Commitment, COP.2.d Commitment, COP.2.e Commitment, COP.2.f Achievement, COP.2.g Commitment, COP.2.h Commitment, COP.2.i Commitment, COP.2.j Excellence, COP.2.k Commitment.
   FOUR OEs CARRY THE ASTERISK -- COP.2.b, COP.2.c, COP.2.f and COP.2.k. The draft builds four separate deep blocks (step 2 for b; step 3 for c; step 6 for f; step 11 for k). COP.2.a, d, e, g, h, i and j are unasterisked and are correspondingly Tier 2. COP.2.j is Excellence; Excellence does not promote it to Tier 1.
   Verified three ways on 2026-08-17: scripts/asterisk_extract.py re-run against the freshly downloaded PDF (self-validation passed; output matched committed scripts/shco_oe_asterisks.json on all 408 entries), the COP.2 pages read directly from /tmp/cop_pages.txt, and the committed asterisk file. COP.2.c WAS among the 14 mismatches of the 2026-08-10 audit (now asterisked, matching the PDF).

TIERING UNDER THE STANDING RULE
1. Two-tier depth standing rule of 2026-08-10 applies. FOUR OF ELEVEN OEs ARE TIER 1. Tier 1: COP.2.b, COP.2.c, COP.2.f, COP.2.k -- procedure steps 2, 3, 6 and 11 carry the reasoning (why treatment is not delayed for police, why a triage stamp without a changed pathway is not a system, why an unmeasured indicator list is not a quality-assurance programme, why an untested NDMA copy is not a disaster plan). Tier 2: COP.2.a (step 1), COP.2.d (step 4), COP.2.e (step 5), COP.2.g (step 7), COP.2.h (step 8), COP.2.i (step 9), COP.2.j (step 10, Excellence) -- requirement and method without extended rationale. Reviewer to note the shallower treatment of a, d, e, g, h, i and j is a DECISION UNDER THE STANDING RULE, not an omission.

CROSS-REFERENCE AND OVERLAP CHECK
2. Tier 1 cross-check (2026-08-17) of COP.2.b/c/f/k against approved HIC.1-HIC.6 and AAC.1 masters, plus unapproved AAC.2, AAC.3, AAC.7, AAC.8 drafts in /tmp/aac_drafts/. Search terms: emergency, triage, ambulance, medico-legal, MLC, disaster, epidemic, outbreak, dead on arrival, discharge note, transfer.
   AAC.2 (unapproved): owns not turning the emergency patient away untreated, unique identification number, admission, bed non-availability, between-organisation transfer. Scope and steps 2, 5, 8 of this draft restate that division and do not rewrite AAC.2. AAC.2.e already writes a triage or prioritisation method at the emergency area and out-patient department for ACCESS. COP.2.c writes ED triage that guides INITIATION OF APPROPRIATE CARE. Division stated in Scope and step 3. FLAG for reconciliation: AAC.2.e and COP.2.c may share a local tool; AAC.2.e's expected time-to-assessment per category must not be read as a COP.2 numeric mandate, and COP.2.c must not silently take over AAC.2.e's OPD prioritisation.
   AAC.3 (unapproved): emergency initial assessment dataset completed in the emergency area; AAC.3.e ward early-warning, not ED triage. Division stated in Scope and step 3. COP.2.d waiting-patient reassessment is not AAC.3.d interval reassessment. FLAG (T2 quick check): COP.2.d vs AAC.3.d/e -- waiting ED vs accepted in-patient; stated in Scope, not a contradiction.
   AAC.8 (unapproved): leaving-the-organisation discharge summary. COP.2.e is the emergency-episode note. Division stated in Scope and step 5. FLAG (T2): AAC.8 Scope does not yet name the COP.2 emergency note; pick up when AAC.8 is reconciled.
   AAC.7 (unapproved): internal transfer and handover method. Ambulance-to-ED receipt is this document's (step 10, chapter ref 45). FLAG (T2): Reay EMS-to-ED handover vs AAC.7 unit-to-unit -- stated in step 10.
   HIC.1 (approved): HIC.1.d IEC, community outbreaks/pandemics, statutory notification. HIC.1 step 24 already contains operational surge, screening, continuation-of-services and drill language. This draft's step 11 takes operational all-hazards command and states that HIC.1 step 24 is the IPC component, not a substitute. FLAG for reconciliation: HIC.1.d's operational surge language now overlaps COP.2.k's owned operational plan; HIC.1 should keep IPC + notification + IEC, COP.2.k should own operational command/surge/disaster triage/continuation. Do not patch HIC.1 in this pass (HIC.1 is approved).
   HIC.2 (approved): standard precautions, hand hygiene, PPE apply in ED and ambulance. Scope and steps 9 and 11 point and do not rewrite. Not a contradiction.
   HIC.3 (approved): BMW generated in ED/ambulance enters the hospital-wide programme. Pointed, not restated. BMW Rules 2016 are NOT in paragraph 2 of the disclaimer.
   HIC.5 (approved): alert-organism notification to ICT. Not this document.
   HIC.6 (approved): reprocessing of reusable ambulance/resuscitation devices. Pointed from step 9; COP.3 will own kit availability.
   AAC.1 (approved): service directory. This draft aligns emergency area and ambulance access to it and does not rewrite it. FLAG (T2): AAC.1.b emergency cover of a defined specialty vs COP.2.a identified emergency area -- resourcing vs place; stated in Scope.
3. FORWARD REFERENCES CREATED BY THIS DRAFT: resuscitation protocol -- COP.3, not yet drafted; intensive-care admission criteria -- COP.6, not yet drafted; two identifiers -- COP.1, not yet drafted; medication process for emergency drugs -- MOM, not yet drafted; facility fire and building evacuation -- FMS, not yet drafted; medical-record structure -- IMS, not yet drafted; consent method -- PRE, not yet drafted; human-resource credentialing -- HRM, not yet drafted. Each is a deliberate boundary.
4. T2 QUICK CHECK (not a full cross-reference audit): COP.2.a identified area vs AAC.1.b emergency cover -- flagged in Scope. COP.2.d waiting reassessment vs AAC.3.d/e -- flagged in Scope. COP.2.e ED note vs AAC.8 summary vs AAC.2.f transfer note -- flagged in Scope and in item 2. COP.2.g DOA vs AAC.8 death case-summary and RBD Act certificate -- flagged in step 7. COP.2.h-i ambulance vs AAC.2.f transfer vs AAC.7 internal move vs MOM medications vs HIC.6 reprocessing -- flagged in steps 8-9. COP.2.j in-transit treatment vs AAC.7 handover -- flagged in step 10.

STATUTORY AND EXTERNAL FACTS
5. Disaster Management Act, 2005 -- cited as the national statute under which disaster authorities may issue directions, and as parent of NDMA guidelines (chapter ref 36). No section number. No assertion which NDMA guideline edition {{HOSPITAL_NAME}} must adopt as a numeric checklist.
6. Epidemic Diseases Act, 1897 as amended by the Epidemic Diseases (Amendment) Act, 2020 -- cited for epidemic directions under the operational plan. Statutory notification remains HIC.1. No section number.
7. Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 and corresponding State medico-legal rules -- cited for MLC documentation and police intimation. The Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 is explicitly NOT cited as if still in force. No BNSS section number is invented. Which State medico-legal rules apply is [Hospital to define].
8. Pt. Parmanand Katara v Union of India (1989) -- used only to inform that emergency care is not delayed for legal formality. Not named in disclaimer paragraph 2 (not a statute).
9. National Medical Commission Act, 2019 -- professional obligation of registered practitioners to provide emergency care; credentialing method remains HR. Not named in disclaimer paragraph 2 (brief's P2 statutes are DMA, EDA, BNSS).
10. ESI / Christ 2010 -- recognised triage frameworks the hospital MAY use. NOT mandated. NO ESI level times, ATS minutes, or other numeric waiting-time thresholds appear as requirements.
11. Byrne 2015 -- informs that DOA needs a defined method. NO numeric definition of "a few minutes" or of DOA.
12. Clinical Establishments Act, 2010 is NOT cited. Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, 2016 are NOT cited in P2. Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 is NOT cited.
13. NO NUMBERS ARE STATED as requirements -- no TAT minutes, no staffing ratios, no ESI times, no surge-bed percentages, no drill-hour lengths, no medication-count minima. Every such value is [Hospital to define]. Consistent with the no-numbers default.

EDITORIAL POSITIONS TAKEN
14. Step 2's rule that treatment is not delayed for police or for a medico-legal form, and that CrPC 1973 is not cited as current, are editorial positions required by Parmanand Katara and by the BNSS succession; the standard requires consonance with statute and written guidance, not this wording.
15. Step 3's rule that a category which does not change the pathway is not a system of triage, and the split from AAC.2.e, are editorial positions; the standard requires that initiation of appropriate care is guided by a system of triage.
16. Step 6's refusal to mandate numeric emergency indicators, and the rule that an unmeasured list is not a programme, are editorial positions; the standard requires that a quality-assurance programme is implemented.
17. Step 11's all-hazards operational plan, the refusal to import NDMA as a numeric checklist, and the split from HIC.1.d, are editorial positions; the standard requires a documented plan for community emergencies, epidemics and other disasters.

DISCLAIMER BLOCK -- STATUTE-MATCHED UNDER THE 2026-08-17 STANDING RULE
18. Paragraphs 1, 3 and 4 are the shared HIC.3-6 block, hash-checked at build time. Paragraph 2 names the Disaster Management Act, 2005, the Epidemic Diseases Act, 1897 as amended 2020, and the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 and corresponding State medico-legal rules -- the statutes this document's References actually rely on. It does NOT name the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, 2016, the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006, or the Clinical Establishments Act, 2010. The HIC wholesale inherit is refused by the build.

DELIBERATELY NOT INCLUDED
- Hospital-wide access prioritisation / OPD triage -- AAC.2.e.
- Between-organisation transfer note -- AAC.2.f.
- Emergency initial-assessment dataset -- AAC.3.
- Ward early-warning -- AAC.3.e.
- Internal unit-to-unit transfer -- AAC.7.
- Leaving-the-organisation discharge summary -- AAC.8.
- Standard precautions, hand hygiene, PPE sequences -- HIC.2.
- Statutory notification and IPC outbreak programme -- HIC.1.d.
- Four-colour BMW -- HIC.3.
- Resuscitation algorithm, joule doses, kit locations -- COP.3.
- Intensive-care admission criteria -- COP.6.
- A numeric ESI / ATS time table.
- CrPC 1973 as if still in force.
- The five optional sections are left unset, matching HIC.1-6 and AAC.1.

HOSPITAL-SPECIFIC VALUES LEFT AS [Hospital to define] -- 26 fillable blanks in the rendered document: 2 in the exact form "[Hospital to define]" (one in Abbreviations, one inside the shared Disclaimer block) and 24 in the guidance-bearing form "[Hospital to define - what to state]". A search for the exact string finds 2 of 26; a search for "Hospital to define" without brackets finds all 26, and that is the search a hospital should be told to run. The figure is produced by policy_placeholder_audit.py across every rendered field in both forms, which also asserts that no nested placeholder exists.

The values the hospital must supply: the identified emergency area, how it is reached, and the resources that back it; the written list of presentations treated as medico-legal cases; who is authorised to complete medico-legal documentation including out of hours; how police are intimated and where the intimation is recorded; the corresponding State medico-legal rules; where the medico-legal register and written emergency-care guidance are held; the triage system, its categories, and who applies it; where the written triage guidance is held; the reassessment interval for each triage category while waiting; the emergency discharge-note content and who gives it; the named emergency quality lead; the emergency quality-assurance indicators and how they are measured; the forum and interval at which emergency quality-assurance findings are reviewed; the procedure for dead-on-arrival and death within minutes; how ambulance services are accessed and that they match the defined scope; the ambulance equipment and emergency-medication list and who is trained to operate the ambulance; how ambulance equipment and medications are checked and how often; how the emergency department initiates treatment while the patient is in transit; the documented disaster, epidemic and community-emergency plan including who activates it; how surge capacity and continuation of essential services are created under the plan; the drill or tabletop interval for the disaster plan; the audit interval for emergency, ambulance and disaster records; the review interval for this policy; the intranet or folder location; and any additional local abbreviation.$q$,
